<h2 id="decision-heading">Which Live Roller Technology Do You Need?</h2>
<h3>Start with Product Weight</h3>
<p>Product weight is the first and most decisive filter. Products under 250 lbs qualify for any live roller technology including MDR. Products from 250-500 lbs narrow the field to BDLR. Products over 500 lbs require <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a>.</p>
<h3>Do You Need Accumulation?</h3>
<p>If yes, <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> provides the simplest and most reliable <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">zero-pressure accumulation</a>. BDLR with pneumatic zones is the traditional alternative. Line-shaft provides light passive accumulation through O-ring slip. CDLR does not accumulate (continuous positive drive).</p>
<h3>Is Noise a Constraint?</h3>
<p>MDR operates at 55-65 dB, conversational level. BDLR runs at 70-80 dB. Line-shaft and CDLR run at 75-85 dB. Facilities with hundreds of feet of conveyor in occupied work areas benefit significantly from MDR's quiet operation.</p>
<h3>Do You Need Diverting?</h3>
<p>Line-shaft conveyors work best with pop-up <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer</a> diverters because individual rollers can be disengaged from the shaft at the transfer point without affecting the rest of the conveyor. BDLR and MDR also work with transfers but require different zone control approaches at the divert point.</p>
<h3>Budget: Upfront vs Total Cost</h3>
<p>BDLR and line-shaft cost less per foot upfront ($$). MDR and CDLR cost more ($$$). However, MDR's on-demand energy use (zones only power when product is present) and lower maintenance (no belts, chains, or O-rings to replace) typically deliver a lower total cost of ownership over the conveyor's 15-20 year lifespan. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> breaks down these factors.</p>
<h3>Mixed Systems</h3>
<p>Most <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a> combine multiple live roller technologies. A typical <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> might use MDR for accumulation zones, BDLR for high-speed transport sections, <a href="/conveyor-types/pallet-conveyors/">CDLR for pallet handling</a>, and <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> for take-away and manual stations. Matching frame and roller dimensions across the technologies keeps product transitions clean as it crosses section boundaries.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Live Roller Applications</h2>
<p><strong>End-of-line packaging:</strong> BDLR and MDR transport sealed cases between case sealers, labelers, and palletizers. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation zones</a> buffer cases before the palletizer. CDLR handles loaded pallets between the palletizer and stretch wrapper.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution and fulfillment:</strong> MDR dominates new distribution center installations for tote and carton transport with ZPA. Line-shaft conveyors remain common in legacy systems and diverter-intensive layouts.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage:</a></strong> Stainless steel BDLR and MDR conveyors handle cased goods in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary</a> and washdown environments. Open-frame construction and sealed bearings support cleaning protocols.</p>
<p><strong>Pallet handling:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> is the only live roller technology rated for full pallet loads. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet conveyor systems</a> use CDLR for transport, right-angle chain <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfers</a> for routing, and <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">VRCs</a> for vertical transport.</p>
<p><strong>Assembly and manufacturing:</strong> Live roller conveyors transport work-in-process between assembly stations. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing configurations</a> stop and start products at each station for manual or robotic operations.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a live roller conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Any <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> where rollers are actively powered. The four technologies are BDLR (belt-driven), line-shaft, <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> (chain-driven), and <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> (motor inside roller).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between CDLR and BDLR?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>CDLR drives every roller positively with chain and sprockets and handles pallets and heavy loads up to 10,000 lbs at 20-120 FPM. BDLR drives rollers by friction from a flat belt underneath and handles cases, cartons, and totes up to 500 lbs at 50-200 FPM. BDLR supports accumulation with belt lifters. CDLR does not accumulate as standard.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">BDLR vs line-shaft, what is the difference?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>BDLR uses a belt under rollers (friction drive, up to 500 lbs). Line-shaft uses a splined shaft with O-rings (up to 300 lbs, natural slip-clutch). BDLR handles heavier loads. Line-shaft suits diverting and light accumulation. See <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyors</a> for details.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">Should I choose MDR or belt-driven live roller?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>MDR for zone control, ZPA, energy efficiency, and low noise (under 250 lbs). BDLR for heavier loads (250-500 lbs), continuous transport, or lower upfront budget. MDR delivers lower total cost of ownership over 15-20 years.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What is belt driven live roller used for?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>BDLR transports cases, cartons, and totes at 50-200 FPM in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a> and distribution centers. It handles up to 500 lbs and supports <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> with pneumatic zone add-ons.</p>

</div>
</div>
